A||||22||Mach10||Artificial heart||||14||
Z||000000||Mach10||08-21-2001||05:23 PM||||I'm glad that medical science has gotten to this point. Probably not as glad as the guy who got it just over a month ago. <P>However, the thing that makes my stomach queasy is the statement that the patient has released to the press: "The heart feels heavier than my old heart. That, and you have to get used to not having a heart-beat anymore. Just this whirring noise that you can hear without a stethescope."<BR>That is just FREAKY.<P>Of course, I move in here to raise a nice controversial issue. Partly, because it's an interesting subject, and partly because nothing fun has happened in O/T since the "Uncle Sam Pays China" thread (thanks MJ! That was fun!)<P>So, how far is far enough? I mean in terms of both Prosthetic replacement, and immortality? Don't get me wrong. I'm happy that this gentleman has a good chance at a longer life. However, at what point to we decide that "No, you are not a candidate to prolong your life?" When is it justifiable to sign a death-warrant on a person? <BR>We can't live forever. Even WITH the technology to do so, we could not allow each other to live forever. Space exploration and expansion aside, there simply wouldn't be room for everyone. You could not have children, as they wouldn't die either, the same as their parents, their parent's parents and so on. Accidental death aside, there would be nothing limiting population. We'd run out of food and space. How does one solve this problem? Set laws stating that life-prolonging treatments be stopped at 100years? 200 years? 300? 50? Where can we draw the line? What religious implications are inherent? What about quality of life? What about the people who could not afford the treatments. Do we WANT to live in a world run by 500-year-old aristocrats? <P>Fire at will.||||reg||14
Z||000001||JohnnyK||08-21-2001||05:32 PM||||Let science run it's course until we have the technology. Why stop now? Sort of like starting a manned mission to mars and then turning back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||08-21-2001||05:39 PM||||Fair enough. The thing is, it's harder to forget something than it is to not learn it. For the mars Analogy, how about this scenario:<P>"Houston, we're 3/4 done our journey to mars!"<BR>"Turn around NOW!"<BR>"but, but why?!? We've come so far!"<BR>"New satelite and scanner results show that mars is infested with huge 600ft tall carniverous dinosaur-like reptiles."<BR>*pause*<BR>"Already 1/4 of the way back to Earth, sir!"<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000003||Steve Normington||08-21-2001||05:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Before he can return home, Tools must gain at least 20 pounds, undergo physical therapy and become fully adept at maintaining the device, which is powered by an external battery pack.<BR>Power comes from a battery pack worn outside the body.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The AbioCor is the first mechanical device designed to replace the entire heart that is totally self-contained within the chest and not connected to outside power or pumps.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is it just me or does the last statement seem at odds with the first two?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||08-21-2001||05:48 PM||||Insert "Futurama" Quote:<BR>"Are you jacking on in there?"<BR>"...NO!!"<P>I wouldn't want an internal battery system. Open-heart surgery if the battery commits suicide?!? Easier to plug in a new system. Maybe have the battery pack located elsewhere, internally, so that you don't trip over a power-cord. That would be fatal...||||reg||7
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||08-21-2001||07:03 PM||||I wish I  could live for at least twenty thousand years at 42 years of age  and I'am already trying to figure out what I am going to be missing out in the future I think medical science has come along way in the last oh 40 years I think if I were born now that in the next 40 years people will be living very easily 150 years.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||08-21-2001||07:11 PM||||Heh... Me too. But I can see the problems inherent...||||reg||7
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||08-21-2001||08:37 PM||||What problems?maybe one child per couple?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||08-21-2001||08:57 PM||||Two problems with that: <BR>1) Everyone in a "Free" nation will go APE ****  if you try to limit what is seen as a natural right.<BR>2) That doesn't solve the problem. No-one dies, remember? Even just 1 child per couple means the population will STILL be growing exponentially. Just at a slower rate.||||reg||7
Z||000009||maryjane||08-21-2001||09:40 PM||||There is something we are all forgetting here. How many of you have known an older person that is fairly good health but has made the comment that they are ready to die? <BR>I've noticed this a lot and it used to bug me till I got older. It sems people just get tired of living. I don't think immortality would be all it's cracked up to be. You'd get bored after about 150 yrs I would think.<BR>Course, I might feel differently if I were close to death. Wonder when Social Security would kick in? 500 yrs?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||08-22-2001||01:33 AM||||I guess that the idea would be that everyone stays 35 years old forever. You know, after your body's done ALL of it's growing, changing etc, but before things start to deteriorate. The only way that I'd want to live forever is if some decent form of space-travel is perfected. Then I'd go for one complete lap around the universe. Then I'd fly into a black hole. That sounds like fun. I wonder if Singularities dream of electron Sheep?||||reg||6
Z||000011||JohnnyK||08-22-2001||01:36 AM||||Well, there are two answers to that. I really used to want to die. But now it seems I need more life if I want to live it to it's fullest. I can't accomplish all I want to with a measly 80 years. I've been trying to devise a way to steal other peoples life.<p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||08-22-2001||01:42 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">Man, am *I* Glad I live in Winnipeg, and not in Guelph!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000013||Mach10||08-22-2001||01:47 AM||||Ick, I just thought of something sticky...<P><BR>Let's assume that, for example:<P>1) Joe is a devout Christian<BR>2) We have the technology to prolong life indefinitely<BR>3) It's affordable, and comes in Pill form, under the trademark Prolong. The prescription comes in 1000year packages<P>Ok, so the Christian Faith has a lot to say about suicide. Joe does the Prolong thing for a while, and lives to 700 years old. He's seen everything, and is tired of living. He decides not to go in for his Prolong treatment. He gets old in 2 days and dies.<P>Now, has Joe committed suicide by opting out on the technique that is easily in his reach? He has the pills, all he had to do was take one from his medicine cabinet.<BR>Does he go to hell? DID he commit suicide by not taking the pill with the intent to die? <BR>||||reg||8
Z||000014||EdsB52||08-22-2001||01:49 AM||||Anyone seen Logans Run or Soilent Green? The answer lies within those two movies!!! Not really  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||08-22-2001||01:55 AM||||Mach10, is that even a valid question?  Thats like saying "You had a miscarriage because of too much stress, therefore its an abortion." Silly.<P>Back to the topic (I know much of you enjoy christian bashing but bear with me here) on hand.  I think as long as there is money in keeping people alive, we will see all these new drugs/procedures to keep everyone living/breathing/moving as long as possible.  For every day John Doe lives on, someone is making a buck.  Whether its right or wrong, I haven't really decided, but if there is one thing I have learned, it's that greed tends to cloud peoples judgements. I will start from there.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Pontiaddict||08-22-2001||02:04 AM||||Not in my opinion, Mach10.<BR>It would be like someone now signing a Do Not Recessitate order. You're not ending your life, you are putting your life back in the hands of fate and not in the hands of doctors.<P>I don't think there's ever going to be a way to cheat death indefinately anyway. Killing people is what humanity does best. <P>Say there's a global war, if someone launches a nuke, no medical procedure is going to keep you alive after being vaporized. Or one faction could tamper with the other sides medicine and make it so it doesn't work. Or the world starts being overpopulated so the government says that you have to be castrated before you get the procedure. Then they screw up and you die geting the operation.<P>This is kind of an offbeat example.<BR>How about the Q on Star Trek? They had the technology to live indefinately and they still had people die after the other Q took their powers away.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Pontiaddict||08-22-2001||02:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Anyone seen Logans Run or Soilent Green? The answer lies within those two movies!!! Not really   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know what Soilent Green is made out of, but it's just sooooooooooo good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Never saw the movies but I did hear the jist of that one.||||reg||1
Z||000018||maryjane||08-22-2001||02:25 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Anyone seen Logans Run or Soilent Green? The answer lies within those two movies!!! Not really    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The original Star Trek series had an episode that was related to this. There was a planet that only the upper class had any space. When you went onto the street, it was people-elbow to elbow, just shuffling about. No wars, disease, or hunger. A miserable existance. Also an old Twilight Zone. You could get a "new", young body for several $K.<BR>Problem was, the elderly couple involved could only afford for one of them to get it. The man became young and came out to see his 65 yr old wife.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Later, he went back & had it reversed.<BR>What if near immortality could be had , but at such a price only some could afford it? Would they look down on the others? Or vice versa? If you stayed 35 for ever would you be<BR>considered the youth (even tho you were really a hundred and fifty)? " Oh, Don look at that terrible old man, Why, he must be at least 70. Why doesn't he just go ahead and die and get it over with. There ought to be a law!"  Things would get boring, even the most exciting things after a while. I remember on my 7 days R&R..... Another story-another time. (Now if you could get a new brain with different emotions and memories, that could make a long life interesting.)<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierohoho||08-22-2001||10:04 AM||||Lets not forget that you couldn't retire at 65. If you live longer you would eventually run out of retirement funds. <P>So I guess it's another ? years in the work force.<P>I don't think I would get tired, I look at all the cool changes technologically since I was born,<BR>Color TV<BR>Velcro<BR>Man Walking on the moon<BR>Photos from the suface of Mars<BR>Fieros<BR>Wonder Bras<BR>Home Computers<BR>I think we will see enough new stiff in the years to come to keep life very interesting if we just don't kill ourselves.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||08-22-2001||01:26 PM||||Black88GT: Yes, I believe so. NOT taking the pill is a concious decision that Joe KNOWS will kill him. I think this is a valid argument. I don't think it's the same thing as the stress-miscarriage.<BR>But on the other hand:<P>Pontiaddict nailed it on the head, and made the point that I missed at 2am *yawn*...<BR>Leaving things up to nature could not be labelled suicide. After 2 days of accelerated aging, Joe would have died of Old Age. No crime in that.<P>I agree that there will always be other forms of death. There isn't a cure for vaporization in pill form (yet). However, the idea is that death by disease or aging will no longer occur.<P>I must admit, that I wouldn't mind an extended life-span. Say, 200 years. Slow things down after the age of 25, so that in 100 Earth-years, your metabolic age would be no more than 50. That way you could still enjoy stuff. Oh, and when I go, I either want it to be a 45,000ft plunge from an airplane, or a quick painless death with my friends/family. Deathbed thing. Can say my goodbye's and such  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||08-22-2001||01:53 PM||||Fundementalist christians believe your soul and your spirit are eternal, only the body you have now is temporary.<P>your soul is the essence of what you are<P>your body is the means by which you interact with the physical world<P>and your spirit is the means by which you interact with the spiritual world (God)<P>  which is why they go on and on that you must be born again - you were born physically, now your spirit must be born - otherwise when your body dies you cannot interact with anything.<P>  but they also teach that, just as Jesus was resserected, all christains will be too at some point in the future. Jesus wasnt simply brought back to life, He was given a new form of body that is eternal and will never age or die.<P>  So why Joe Christian would want to keep this ratty old body alive for a thousand years or more makes no sense from his perspective - It would be like finding a way to stay in your mothers womb for a 100 years, instead of being born - why would you want to?!<P>  Christians dont fear death, in a way they look forward to it, just like a serious student looks forward to getting out of college and out into the real world.<P>  the student understands there are reasons why he needs to be in college, so he cant just quit - likewise there are reasons we need to be here now, so we dont end our lives when we feel like it. But extend our lives for a thousand years?! No thanks!||||reg||10
